problems.problem:
Antonio and Bernardo play the following game. There are two piles of tokens, one with $m$ tokens and the other with $n$ tokens. In turn, each player makes one of the following moves:

• take one token from one of the piles;

• take one token from each of the piles;

• move one token from one pile to the other.

A player who cannot move loses. Antonio starts. Determine, in terms of $m$ and $n$, whether one of the players has a winning strategy, and if so, which one.

Determine whether the following statement is true or false:

For every sequence $x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots$ of non-negative real numbers there exist two sequences $a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots$ and $b_1, b_2, b_3, \dots$ of non-negative real numbers such that

• $x_n = a_n + b_n$ for every $n$;

• $a_1 + \cdots + a_n \le n$ for infinitely many values of $n$;

• $b_1 + \cdots + b_n \le n$ for infinitely many values of $n$ (possibly different from the previous ones).

Let $ABCDEFGHILMN$ be a regular dodecagon. Let $P$ be the intersection of the diagonals $AF$ and $DH$. Let $S$ be the circle through $A$ and $H$ that is congruent to the circumcircle of the dodecagon and distinct from it. Prove that:

(a) $P$ lies on $S$;

(b) the center of $S$ lies on the diagonal $HN$;

(c) the length of $PE$ equals the side of the dodecagon.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying simultaneously the following properties:

• for every pair of integers $(m, n)$ with $m < n$, $f(m) < f(n)$;

• for every pair of integers $(m, n)$ there is an integer $k$ such that $f(m) - f(n) = f(k)$.

Let $ABC$ be a triangle with all angles greater than $45^\circ$ and less than $90^\circ$.

(a) Prove that three squares with the following properties can be placed inside $ABC$:

• the three squares all have the same side;

• the three squares have a common vertex $K$ inside the triangle;

• any two of the squares have no common point other than $K$;

• each square has two opposite vertices on the perimeter of $ABC$ and otherwise lies entirely inside triangle $ABC$.

(b) Let $P$ be the center of the square with side $AB$ constructed outside $ABC$. Let $r_C$ be the reflection of the line $CK$ in the bisector of angle $\angle ACB$. Prove that $P$ lies on $r_C$.

A positive integer $n$ is called pleasant if it has the following properties:

• its decimal representation consists of $4$ digits;

• the first and the third digits of $n$ are equal;

• the second and the fourth digits of $n$ are equal;

• the product of the digits of $n$ divides $n^2$.

Find all pleasant numbers.

Every non-negative integer (zero included) is coloured white or red so that:

• there is at least one white number and at least one red number;

• the sum of a white number and a red number is white;

• the product of a white number and a red number is red.

Prove that the product of two red numbers is always red and that the sum of two red numbers is always red.

In the land of Cockaigne the following solitaire game is played. One starts from a finite string of zeros and ones, and the following moves are allowed:

(i) delete two consecutive ones;

(ii) delete three consecutive zeros;

(iii) if the string contains the substring $01$, replace this substring by $100$.

The moves are made one at a time, in succession. The game is won if the string is reduced to one of two digits or fewer. (For example, starting from $0101$ one can win by first applying move (iii) to the last two digits, getting $01100$, then move (i) to its two ones, and finally move (ii) to the three remaining zeros, getting the empty string.) Among all $1024$ possible strings of ten digits, how many are there from which it is not possible to win?

Let $X = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\}$. We want to colour all $3$-element subsets of $X$ with $k$ colours so that any two disjoint subsets have different colours. Prove that:

(a) $4$ colours are sufficient;

(b) $3$ colours are not sufficient.

A model car is tested on some closed circuits $600$ metres long, made up of flat stretches and of uphill or downhill stretches. All uphill and downhill stretches have the same slope. The tests reveal some curious facts:

(a) the speed of the car depends only on whether it is going uphill, on the flat or downhill; calling these three speeds $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$ respectively, we have $v_s < v_p < v_d$;

(b) $v_s$, $v_p$ and $v_d$, expressed in metres per second, are integers;

(c) however the circuit is made up (with more or fewer climbs and descents), the time needed to complete it is always $50$ seconds.

Find all possible values of $v_s$, $v_p$, $v_d$.

Let $ABC$ be an isosceles triangle with $AB = AC$ and $\angle BAC < 60^\circ$. Let $D$ be the point on $AC$ such that $\angle DBC = \angle BAC$, let $E$ be the intersection of the perpendicular bisector of $BD$ with the line through $A$ parallel to $BC$, and let $F$ be the point on the line $AC$, on the opposite side of $A$ from $C$, such that $FA$ is twice as long as $AC$. Finally, let $r$ be the perpendicular to $AB$ through $F$, $s$ the perpendicular to $AC$ through $E$, and $t$ the line $BD$. Prove that:

(a) the lines $EB$ and $AC$ are parallel;

(b) the lines $r$, $s$ and $t$ are concurrent.

Let $ABC$ be a triangle such that, if $H$ is the foot of the altitude from $C$, then $AH = 3 \cdot HB$. Moreover, let:

• $M$ be the midpoint of $AB$;

• $N$ be the midpoint of $AC$;

• $P$ be the point on the opposite side of line $AC$ from $B$ such that $NP = NC$ and $PC = CB$.

Prove that $\angle APM = \angle PBA$.

Find all pairs $(a, b)$ of positive integers satisfying the following three conditions:

• $b > a$ and $b - a$ is a prime;

• the units digit of $a + b$ is $3$;

• $ab$ is the square of an integer.

Let $S$ be the set of integers greater than or equal to $2$. A function $f : S \to S$ is called primordial if it has the following properties:

• it is surjective (i.e. for every $s \in S$ there is at least one $n \in S$ with $f(n) = s$);

• it is increasing on primes (i.e. if $p_1 < p_2$ are primes, then $f(p_1) < f(p_2)$);

• for every $n \in S$, $f(n)$ is the product of $f(p)$ over all primes $p$ dividing $n$ (so, for example, $f(360) = f(2^3 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5) = f(2) \cdot f(3) \cdot f(5)$).

Determine the largest and the smallest possible value of $f(2020)$ as $f$ ranges over all primordial functions.

A positive integer $m$ is called a zero-carrier if there is a positive integer $k$ such that:

• $k$ is a perfect square;

• $k$ is a multiple of $m$;

• the decimal representation of $k$ contains at least $2021$ digits $0$, but its last digit (the rightmost one) is different from $0$.

Find all zero-carriers.

Let $ABC$ be an acute triangle, let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$, and let $H$ be the foot of the altitude from $B$. Let $Q$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ABM$, and let $X$ be the intersection of the altitude $BH$ with the perpendicular bisector of $BC$. Prove that the following two facts are equivalent:

(i) the circumcircle of triangle $ACM$, the circumcircle of triangle $AXH$ and the line $CQ$ pass through a common point;

(ii) the lines $BQ$ and $CQ$ are perpendicular.

Determine for which positive integers $n$ there is a positive integer $A$ such that:

• $A$ is a multiple of $2022$;

• the decimal representation of $A$ contains only the digits $0$ and $7$;

• the decimal representation of $A$ contains the digit $7$ exactly $n$ times.

Let $ABC$ be an acute triangle with $AB < AC$. Let:

• $D$ be the foot of the bisector of the angle at $A$;

• $E$ be the point of segment $BC$ (different from $B$) such that $AB = AE$;

• $F$ be the point of segment $BC$ (different from $B$) such that $BD = DF$;

• $G$ be the point of segment $AC$ such that $AB = AG$.

Prove that the circumcircle of triangle $EFG$ is tangent to the line $AC$.
